# frozen_string_literal: true

=begin

  Logs the game to a file, in GemStone and DragonRealms: the game text to a .log
  file, with --xml the raw XML the game sends to a .xml file, or with --both both.

  ;log help shows every option.

  Logs go to Lich's log folder (lich/logs, or the one set with Lich's --logs=),
  under <game>-<character>/<year>/<month>/.

       author: elanthia-online
 contributors: Tillmen, Tysong, Xanlin, LostRanger
         game: any
      version: 1.7.0
     required: Lich >= 5.19.0

  changelog:
    1.7.0 (2026-11-12):
      * New --compress-after=N option (or log_compress_after: N in DragonRealms yaml) gzips log files older than N days (0: all but today's).
    1.6.1 (2026-11-12):
      * XML logs no longer drop an lnet chat line that arrives while inventory is listed.
    1.6.0 (2026-11-11):
      * New --both option writes the text log and the XML log at once, from one ;log.
    1.5.0 (2026-11-11):
      * XML logs now also record script output (what other scripts echo), as text logs always have.
    1.4.0 (2026-11-11):
      * ;log help now lists the options, and this header is much shorter.
    1.3.5 (2026-11-11):
      * No false warning about typed commands when Lich stops the logger itself.
    1.3.4 (2026-11-11):
      * logxml.lic is retired: use ;log --xml (an existing logxml.lic keeps working).
    1.3.3 (2026-11-10):
      * Documentation update; logs are unchanged.
    1.3.2 (2026-11-10):
      * XML logs keep prompts and speech that used to be dropped with stream lines.
    1.3.1 (2026-11-10):
      * Room numbers (--rnum) work in XML logs as they do in text logs, including DragonRealms.
    1.3.0 (2026-11-10):
      * New --xml option logs the raw XML, as logxml.lic did, with every option available.
=end
=begin
    1.2.8 (2026-11-10):
      * Internal cleanup; now needs Lich 5.19.0 or later.
    1.2.7 (2026-11-10):
      * Internal cleanup; logs are unchanged.
    1.2.6 (2026-11-10):
      * The logger now says why when it stops logging typed commands or cannot close a file.
    1.2.5 (2026-11-10):
      * Two loggers running at once no longer mix up each other's options.
    1.2.4 (2026-11-10):
      * New files start cleanly at midnight, every file gets its closing time line, and Lich's --logs= folder is used.
    1.2.3 (2026-11-10):
      * --rnum and --exclude no longer send commands to the game or slow it down, and --rnum works in DragonRealms.
    1.2.2 (2026-11-10):
      * --timestamp and --lines no longer crash on commas, odd values or a missing value.
    1.2.1 (2026-09-25):
      * Expand regex match for various XML that may come across due to script output
    1.2.0 (2026-04-23):
      * Use --timestamp format for log file header/footer lines when enabled
    1.1.0 (2026-04-23):
      * Timestamp reget lines with $login_time instead of dumping them raw
      * Filter pushStream/popStream tags from reget output
    1.0.0 (2025-04-08):
      * Fix --rnum to better match only room titles
      * Add --exclude=288,u7199 support to exclude logging in rooms designated.
      * Add --lines=50000 to change default 30k lines captured before starting new log file
      * Convert to module GameLogger
    0.0.8 (2024-09-10):
      * Add --rnum support
    0.0.7 (2023-03-31):
      * Rubocop code cleanup
      * Adjust filename to include hours, minutes, seconds
      * Save into subdirectories by year/month
      * Starts a new file when the day changes
      * Add log timestamp CLI option for non-DragonRealms
    0.0.6 (2023-01-23):
      * Update for Ruby v3 compatibility
    0.0.5 (2022-11-24):
      * Remove reference to $SAFE
    0.0.4 (2022-01-10):
      * Making datetime stamps customizeable, and tuneable in a character's yaml.
    0.0.3 (2022-01-04):
      * prepend datetime stamps to logged lines
    0.0.2 (2015-01-13):
      * create log directory if needed
=end

hide_me

require 'date'
require 'zlib'

# The game logger behind ;log and ;log --xml. Each run of log.lic (and
# of an install's local logxml.lic 0.7.x, which loads this file under the
# name logxml; logxml.lic left the repository in 1.3.4) evaluates this
# module again and then calls {GameLogger.main}, which reads the run's
# options ({Opts.parse}) and runs one +GameLogger::Logger+ in the mode they
# and the script's name pick: text mode, the game text Lich hands scripts,
# to a ".log" file; or XML mode, the raw XML the game sends, to a ".xml"
# file. The header at the top of log.lic describes both modes, every
# option and where the files go, for players.
#
# What is left on the module is what a run uses only while it starts: the
# option parser ({Opts}), the checks that turn the --lines and --timestamp
# options into values ({lines_per_file}, {timestamp_format}), the --exclude
# split ({exclude_entries}) and the background map load
# ({start_map_load}). Everything a run uses while it logs is on its own
# +GameLogger::Logger+ class and its +GameLogger::Mode+ value, which each
# evaluation builds new (see +GameLogger::Mode+).
#
# @note No other script calls this module. spec/scripts/log_spec.rb and
#   spec/scripts/log_xml_mode_spec.rb evaluate it (see
#   spec/support/log_spec_harness.rb) and call {Opts.parse},
#   {GameLogger.main} and the +GameLogger::Logger+ methods.
module GameLogger
  # Lines written to one log file before a new file is started, when
  # --lines is not given or is not a valid count.
  DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE = 30_000 unless defined?(DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE)

  # Timestamp format used when the settings turn timestamps on but give no
  # usable log_timestamp_format (the DR base.yaml default).
  DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT = '%F %T %Z' unless defined?(DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT)

  # A --lines value: plain digits, or groups of three digits after the first
  # group, split by "," or "_" (50000, 50,000, 50_000, 1,000,000).
  LINE_COUNT_PATTERN = /\A(?:[0-9]+|[0-9]{1,3}(?:[,_][0-9]{3})+)\z/ unless defined?(LINE_COUNT_PATTERN)

  # The option {start_xml_twin} sets on the XML twin of --both, which
  # leaves compressing old logs to its text logger. It has spaces, so no
  # argument a player types parses to it.
  TWIN_KEY = :'xml twin of --both' unless defined?(TWIN_KEY)

  # Log files open in this Lich process, by path ({open_log_path}), with how
  # many loggers hold each: every logger for a character runs in this one
  # process (the --both twin and an install's local logxml.lic too), so a
  # sweep skips exactly the files in use ({log_path_open?}). Kept on the
  # module, which each run evaluates again, so all runs share one table.
  OPEN_LOG_PATHS = Hash.new(0) unless defined?(OPEN_LOG_PATHS)

  # Guards {OPEN_LOG_PATHS}: loggers and their sweep threads use it at once.
  OPEN_LOG_PATHS_LOCK = Mutex.new unless defined?(OPEN_LOG_PATHS_LOCK)

  # Records that a logger is about to open +path+, until {close_log_path}.
  #
  # @param path [String] the log file's path
  # @return [String] the path as recorded (expanded)
  def self.open_log_path(path)
    key = File.expand_path(path)
    OPEN_LOG_PATHS_LOCK.synchronize { OPEN_LOG_PATHS[key] += 1 }
    key
  end

  # Records that a logger closed +key+ (as {open_log_path} returned it).
  #
  # @param key [String] the recorded path
  # @return [void]
  def self.close_log_path(key)
    OPEN_LOG_PATHS_LOCK.synchronize do
      OPEN_LOG_PATHS[key] -= 1
      OPEN_LOG_PATHS.delete(key) unless OPEN_LOG_PATHS[key].positive?
    end
  end

  # Whether some logger in this Lich process has +path+ open.
  #
  # @param path [String] a log file's path
  # @return [Boolean]
  def self.log_path_open?(path)
    key = File.expand_path(path)
    OPEN_LOG_PATHS_LOCK.synchronize { OPEN_LOG_PATHS.key?(key) }
  end

  # The script's argument parser: turns ;log's arguments into one option
  # per name, an argument starting with {FLAG_PREFIX} as a flag
  # ({parse_flag}) and any other word as a +true+ option
  # ({parse_command}). It only reads the arguments; what each option does
  # is decided where it is used (`GameLogger::Logger.mode_for` and the
  # run's configure step).
  module Opts
    # What starts a flag argument (--rnum, --lines=50000); any other
    # argument is a positional word.
    FLAG_PREFIX = '--' unless defined?(FLAG_PREFIX)

    # Flags whose value is a comma-separated list. Every other flag keeps its
    # value whole, so a --timestamp format may contain commas.
    LIST_FLAGS = %i[exclude].freeze unless defined?(LIST_FLAGS)

    # Records a positional word as a +true+ option. A word never replaces a
    # value already given for the same name (the word +timestamp+ after
    # --timestamp=FMT keeps FMT).
    #
    # @param h [Hash{Symbol => Object}] options parsed so far; updated in place
    # @param c [String] the positional word, e.g. "streams"
    # @return [String, Array<String>, true] the value now stored under the
    #   word's name: +true+, or the value a flag of that name gave earlier
    def self.parse_command(h, c)
      h[c.to_sym] ||= true
    end

    # Records one --name[=value] flag. The value is everything after the
    # first "="; only {LIST_FLAGS} are split on commas (a single item stays
    # a String). A bare flag is +true+ and never replaces a value already
    # given for the same name; a valued flag replaces any earlier value, so
    # the last --timestamp=FMT wins. A flag with no name (+--+ or +--=x+)
    # echoes and is ignored.
    #
    # @param h [Hash{Symbol => Object}] options parsed so far; updated in place
    # @param f [String] the flag as typed, e.g. "--timestamp=%b %d, %Y %T"
    # @return [String, Array<String>, true, nil] the value now stored for the
    #   flag, or +nil+ when the flag has no name and is ignored
    def self.parse_flag(h, f)
      (name, val) = f[2..-1].split("=", 2)
      if name.nil? || name.empty?
        echo "#{f} has no option name, so it is ignored."
        return nil
      end

      key = name.to_sym
      if val.nil?
        h[key] ||= true
      elsif LIST_FLAGS.include?(key)
        val = val.split(",")

        h[key] = val.size == 1 ? val.first : val
      else
        h[key] = val
      end
    end

    # Parses the script's arguments into options. Arguments starting with
    # +--+ go through {parse_flag}; any other word goes through
    # {parse_command}. With no +args+, reads Script.current.vars: when
    # vars[0] holds a double quote, it is re-split so a quoted value
    # (--timestamp="%F %T %Z") stays one argument, which matters for
    # autostart, whose saved arguments reach vars[1..] split on spaces.
    #
    # @param args [Array<String>, nil] arguments to parse; +nil+ reads
    #   Script.current.vars
    # @return [OpenStruct] one member per option name; an option not given
    #   reads as +nil+
    def self.parse(args = nil)
      case args
      when nil
        if Script.current.vars[0].inspect =~ /\\"/
          args = Script.current.vars[0].scan(/[^\s"]*(?<!\\)"(?:\\"|[^"])+(?<!\\)"[^\s]*|(?:\\"|[^"\s])+/).collect { |s| s.gsub(/(?<!\\)"/, '').gsub('\\"', '"') }
        else
          args = Script.current.vars[1..-1]
        end
      end

      OpenStruct.new(**args.to_a.reduce(Hash.new) do |opts, v|
        if v.start_with?(FLAG_PREFIX)
          Opts.parse_flag(opts, v)
        else
          Opts.parse_command(opts, v)
        end
        opts
      end)
    end
  end

  # Turns the --lines option into a line count. Accepts whole numbers above
  # 0 written as plain digits or in groups of three digits split by "," or
  # "_" (50000, 50,000, 50_000); see {LINE_COUNT_PATTERN}. Anything else (0,
  # negative, words, misplaced separators like 50,00, a bare --lines or the
  # word lines) echoes one message and falls back to
  # {DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE}, so a bad value can neither crash the logger
  # nor make it rotate files without reading lines.
  #
  # @param value [String, true, nil] the parsed --lines option (`true` for a
  #   bare --lines or the word lines, +nil+ when not given)
  # @return [Integer] lines per log file, always above 0
  def self.lines_per_file(value)
    return DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE if value.nil?

    count = Integer(value.delete(',_'), 10) if value.is_a?(String) && value.match?(LINE_COUNT_PATTERN)
    return count if count&.positive?

    problem = value.is_a?(String) ? "--lines=#{value} is not a valid line count" : 'lines was given without a value'
    echo "#{problem}; use a whole number above 0, like --lines=50000. Using the default of #{DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE} lines per file."
    DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE
  end

  # Turns the --compress-after option, or the log_compress_after setting
  # when the option is not given (DragonRealms yaml), into a number of
  # days: log files dated more than that many days before today are
  # gzipped (see +GameLogger::Logger.compress_old_logs+), so 0 means every
  # file dated before today. Today's files are never compressed: another
  # logger (the XML twin of --both, a second ;log) may still have one open.
  # Only a whole number, 0 or above, is a number of days; anything else
  # (negative, words, a bare --compress-after) echoes once and turns
  # compression off.
  #
  # @param option [String, true, nil] the parsed --compress-after option
  # @param setting [Integer, String, nil] settings.log_compress_after
  # @return [Integer, nil] the days, or +nil+ for no compression
  def self.compress_after_days(option, setting)
    value, source = option.nil? ? [setting, 'log_compress_after'] : [option, '--compress-after']
    return nil if value.nil?

    days = value if value.is_a?(Integer)
    days = Integer(value, 10) if value.is_a?(String) && value.match?(/\A[0-9]+\z/)
    return days if days && days >= 0

    problem = value == true ? "#{source} was given without a number of days" : "#{source} #{value.inspect} is not a number of days"
    echo "#{problem}; use a whole number, 0 or above, like --compress-after=7. Old logs are not compressed."
    nil
  end

  # Whether a value can be handed to strftime as a timestamp format: only a
  # non-empty String is.
  #
  # @param value [Object] a --timestamp option or a log_timestamp_format setting
  # @return [Boolean]
  def self.timestamp_format?(value)
    value.is_a?(String) && !value.empty?
  end

  # Picks the timestamp format from the --timestamp option and the profile
  # settings, checking both the same way ({timestamp_format?}) so strftime
  # never sees anything but a format. A --timestamp=FMT wins. A --timestamp
  # with no format (bare flag, the word timestamp, or --timestamp=) echoes
  # once and is ignored. Settings that turn timestamps on without a usable
  # log_timestamp_format echo once and use {DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT}.
  #
  # @param option [String, true, nil] the parsed --timestamp option
  # @param enabled [Object] settings.log_timestamp; truthy turns timestamps on
  #   (`nil` when there are no settings)
  # @param format [Object] settings.log_timestamp_format
  # @return [String, nil] the format to stamp with, or +nil+ for no timestamps
  def self.timestamp_format(option, enabled, format)
    return option if timestamp_format?(option)

    echo 'timestamp was given without a format, so it is ignored; to add timestamps use --timestamp="%F %T %Z".' unless option.nil?
    return nil unless enabled
    return format if timestamp_format?(format)

    echo "log_timestamp is on in your settings, but log_timestamp_format #{format.inspect} is not a format; using #{DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT.inspect}."
    DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT
  end

  # Starts loading the map in a background thread when nothing has loaded
  # it yet, so a Lich room number in --exclude or --rnum can be found. Lich
  # loads the map only when something asks for it, and in DragonRealms its
  # own room display does not by default. The load sends no game command,
  # but it can take seconds, so it runs off the logger's get loop: a logger
  # waiting on it during a login burst would let the script's 400-line
  # downstream buffer (lich-5 lib/common/script.rb) drop lines.
  #
  # @return [Thread, nil] the loading thread, or +nil+ when the map was
  #   already loaded and nothing was started
  def self.start_map_load
    return nil if Map.loaded?

    Thread.new do
      echo "no map database could be loaded, so Lich room numbers in --exclude and --rnum match no room; u<uid> entries still work." unless Map.load
    rescue StandardError => e
      echo "the map failed to load (#{e.message}), so Lich room numbers in --exclude and --rnum match no room; u<uid> entries still work."
    end
  end

  # Splits the --exclude option into game UID entries ("u7199") and Lich
  # room number entries ("288"). Empty entries (--exclude=,288) and a bare
  # --exclude (`true`) exclude nothing.
  #
  # @param option [String, Array<String>, true, nil] the parsed --exclude
  #   option
  # @return [Array(Array<String>, Array<String>)] the "u" UID entries, then
  #   the Lich room number entries
  def self.exclude_entries(option)
    Array(option).grep(String).reject(&:empty?).partition { |entry| entry.start_with?('u') }
  end

  # The throwaway module this evaluation builds Mode and Logger in (as
  # fresh::Mode and fresh::Logger) before each is set on GameLogger, once
  # it is whole (see {Mode}). It is made first, so XML mode's line filter
  # can name the {Logger::StreamSpanFilter} of the Logger built with it.
  fresh = Module.new

  # What one log format decides, as one frozen value. The logging loop
  # ({Logger}) is written once; everything that differs between output
  # formats is a field here, so a format is a value handed to the loop, not
  # a branch in it. There are two formats: text mode ({Mode::TEXT}) and XML
  # mode ({Mode::XML}, and {Mode::XML_STREAMS} for its streams option);
  # {Logger.mode_for} picks one for a run.
  #
  # The proc fields keep no state. {#line_filter} runs once per run and
  # builds the run's line filter, which runs on every line the run reads
  # and may keep state across lines (XML mode's follows stream spans);
  # {#room_title} runs in the logger's get loop, only while --rnum is on,
  # on the lines outside an excluded room, so it stays cheap. Both modes
  # number a room title line with the one decorator,
  # {Logger.number_room_title}; {#uid_after_title} is the one thing they
  # tell it.
  #
  # Mode, {Mode::TEXT} and {Logger} are built new, not reopened, each time
  # Lich evaluates this module (once per log run in a session), and then
  # put in place of the last ones, with no constant warning. A run started
  # after log.lic was updated in the session gets the new definitions, and
  # a run already logging keeps the Mode value and the Logger class it
  # started with, and with the class its file, room and typed-command
  # helpers. A guarded `Mode ||=` would keep the first version's class for
  # the whole session, and a newer log.lic that adds a field would fail to
  # start. Each is built in the throwaway +fresh+ module, and the
  # constants are set only once each definition is whole, so a
  # run starting in another thread meanwhile (;force log racing a start)
  # gets the old definitions or the new ones, never a missing one.
  #
  # @!attribute [r] extension
  #   @return [String] the log file extension with its dot, e.g. ".log"
  # @!attribute [r] header_stamp
  #   @return [Proc, nil] makes the header line when timestamps are off:
  #     called with the Time, returns the line. +nil+ writes the same stamp
  #     as the footer (see {Logger.file_stamp}). With timestamps on,
  #     the header uses the timestamp format whatever this is.
  # @!attribute [r] upstream_line
  #   @return [Proc] called with one typed command as upstream_get returns
  #     it; returns the line logged for it
  # @!attribute [r] script_flags
  #   @return [Hash{Symbol => Boolean}] the Script.current flags the run
  #     sets when it starts, in this order; +want_upstream: true+ sets
  #     Script.current.want_upstream = true. A flag not listed keeps
  #     lich-5's default.
  # @!attribute [r] line_filter
  #   @return [Proc] builds a run's line filter, once per run (see
  #     {Logger#initialize}): called with no arguments, it returns an object
  #     whose +call+ takes each game line the run reads, in order, and
  #     returns the text written for it: the line itself, the part of it
  #     the filter keeps, or +nil+ when nothing of it is written (it then
  #     does not count toward --lines). Each run gets a filter of its own,
  #     as one may keep state from line to line.
  # @!attribute [r] reget_line
  #   @return [Proc] called with one line of reget's answer, the timestamp
  #     format (`nil` for none) and the login Time ($login_time); returns
  #     the line written at the top of the first file
  # @!attribute [r] room_title
  #   @return [Proc] called with a game line and the current room's title
  #     matcher ({Logger.room_title_pattern}; +nil+ when --rnum is off
  #     or before the first room title); truthy when the line is the room's
  #     title line, which --rnum numbers
  # @!attribute [r] uid_after_title
  #   @return [Boolean] where {Logger.number_room_title} puts the GemStone
  #     " (u<uid>)" on a room title line: +true+ right after the title's
  #     first "]", where logxml.lic 0.6.6 put it on GemStone title lines;
  #     +false+ at the end of the line, where log.lic has always put it.
  # @!attribute [r] started_text
  #   @return [String] the echo once logging has started, before the log
  #     file's path (a space between them)
  fresh::Mode = Data.define(:extension, :header_stamp, :upstream_line, :script_flags, :line_filter, :reget_line,
                            :room_title, :uid_after_title, :started_text)
  mode = fresh::Mode

  # Text mode, log.lic's format (Mode::TEXT, described where it is set).
  text = mode.new(
    extension: '.log',
    header_stamp: nil,
    upstream_line: ->(command) { ">#{command.sub(/^<c>/, '')}" },
    script_flags: { want_script_output: true, want_upstream: true }.freeze,
    line_filter: -> { ->(line) { line unless line =~ %r{^</?(?:(?:push|pop)Stream|progressBar|dialogData|(?:open|close)Dialog)} } },
    reget_line: lambda do |line, stamp_format, login_time|
      line = line.chomp
      stamp_format ? "#{login_time.strftime(stamp_format)}: #{line}" : line
    end,
    room_title: ->(line, title_pattern) { title_pattern&.match?(line.b) },
    uid_after_title: false,
    started_text: 'Non-XML Logging started, currently logging to'
  )
  # Mode::TEXT, Mode::XML and Mode::XML_STREAMS, and the three Logger
  # constants after the class, are set with const_set: YARD registers no
  # constant set that way (nor one assigned on a local, mode::TEXT = ...),
  # so each is documented in a @!parse block. YARD files the Mode and
  # Logger built in +fresh+ under the path fresh::Mode and fresh::Logger,
  # so the blocks open those paths, and the constants appear on the class
  # pages with the rest of each class.
  #
  # @!parse
  #   class fresh::Mode
  #     # Text mode, log.lic's format: the game text as lich-5 hands it to a
  #     # script (downstream text, lich-5's default, left on), Lich's echoes too
  #     # (want_script_output), and typed commands (want_upstream) as ">command"
  #     # with a leading "<c>" removed. Files end in ".log". A line that starts
  #     # with a stream, dialog or progress bar tag (script output can carry
  #     # them) is not written. Reget lines lose their "\r" ($_SERVERBUFFER_
  #     # keeps CRLF and reget splits on "\n") and get the $login_time stamp when
  #     # timestamps are on. They are not filtered: this mode never sets
  #     # want_downstream_xml, so reget has already removed every XML tag, and a
  #     # "<" left in a line is game text the server sent escaped as "&lt;"
  #     # (lich-5 lib/global_defs.rb, def reget). The room title line is the
  #     # one that starts with XMLData.room_title (see
  #     # {Logger.room_title_pattern}); its GemStone u-id goes at the end of the
  #     # line.
  #     TEXT = text
  #   end
  mode.const_set(:TEXT, text)

  # XML mode, the format logxml.lic wrote (Mode::XML, described where it
  # is set).
  xml = mode.new(
    extension: '.xml',
    header_stamp: ->(time) { time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %I:%M%P').sub(/0([0-9]+:)/, '\1') },
    upstream_line: ->(command) { "<!-- CLIENT -->#{command}<!-- ENDCLIENT -->" },
    script_flags: { want_script_output: true, want_upstream: true, want_downstream: false, want_downstream_xml: true }.freeze,
    line_filter: -> { fresh::Logger::StreamSpanFilter.new },
    reget_line: ->(line, _stamp_format, _login_time) { line.chomp },
    room_title: ->(line, _title_pattern) { line.match?(%r{\A(?:<resource picture="\d+"/>|<popBold/>)?(?:<style id="roomName"\s+/>|<room>)}) },
    uid_after_title: true,
    started_text: 'XML Logging started, currently logging to'
  )
  # @!parse
  #   class fresh::Mode
  #     # XML mode, the format logxml.lic wrote (selected by --xml, or by a
  #     # run named logxml, see {Logger.mode_for}): the XML lich-5 hands a
  #     # script that wants the XML downstream and not the text downstream,
  #     # Lich's and other scripts' echoes (want_script_output, as text mode;
  #     # logxml.lic left them out), and typed commands as
  #     # "<!-- CLIENT -->command<!-- ENDCLIENT -->"
  #     # (a leading "<c>" kept). Files end in ".xml", and with timestamps off the
  #     # header is the date and a 12-hour time with the hour's leading zero
  #     # dropped ("2026-04-23 9:05am"); the footer is the same as text mode's.
  #     # The stream content lich-5 keeps out of the main-window text it hands
  #     # scripts is left out of each line, and every other byte of the line is
  #     # kept; a line left blank is not written (see {Logger::StreamSpanFilter}).
  #     # The flags are set before reget is called, so reget answers the raw XML
  #     # the server sent; its lines are written as they come, unstamped and
  #     # unfiltered, less their "\r".
  #     #
  #     # --rnum numbers the room title line in the forms lich-5 numbers for its
  #     # own room display (lib/games.rb, process_room_information): at the start
  #     # of the line, an optional picture resource or popBold, then the roomName
  #     # style or a Saga-identity <room> block, such as
  #     # '<resource picture="0"/><style id="roomName" />[Title] (uid)' and
  #     # '<resource picture="0"/><room><name>[Title] (uid)</name>...'. Lich also
  #     # numbers those forms after a familiar pushStream, the familiar's view of
  #     # its own room, which is not the character's room, so this mode does not.
  #     # The form must start the String (\A, not ^): the numbers go at the first
  #     # "]", so a title after a line break in it is not numbered. The numbers
  #     # are {Logger.number_room_title}'s, as in text mode, with the GemStone
  #     # u-id right after the first "]", where logxml.lic 0.6.6 put it. The
  #     # test logxml 0.6.6 and log.lic 1.3.0 used (a line starting with the
  #     # picture resource and naming roomName anywhere after it) also numbered a
  #     # line with the picture resource and then popBold before the style; Lich's
  #     # pattern does not, so neither does this one. None of lich-5's
  #     # spec/fixtures/streams lines has that form.
  #     XML = xml
  #   end
  mode.const_set(:XML, xml)

  # @!parse
  #   class fresh::Mode
  #     # XML mode started with the word streams or --streams: every line is
  #     # written as it comes, stream content too.
  #     XML_STREAMS = XML.with(line_filter: -> { ->(line) { line } })
  #   end
  mode.const_set(:XML_STREAMS, mode::XML.with(line_filter: -> { ->(line) { line } }))

  # One log run, in one {Mode}: all its state (options, settings, the room
  # it is in, the file it writes, its line filter) is in this object, never
  # on the module.
  # {GameLogger.main} builds one per run of the script and runs it.
  #
  # The logic that decides what a line becomes ({#line_text},
  # {#decorate_title}, {#format_line}, {#reget_lines}) is in small methods
  # that read their arguments, the run's settings, its current room and
  # XMLData.game; {#line_text} also goes through the run's line filter,
  # which in XML mode carries from one line to the next whether a stream
  # span is open (see {StreamSpanFilter}). Every time the run uses comes
  # from the +clock+ given to {#initialize}, and the game lines, reget, the start echo and the
  # settings come from its +io+, so a spec can feed a run lines and a time
  # without stubbing Time. The rest still goes through Lich: the echoes
  # from {GameLogger.timestamp_format}, {GameLogger.lines_per_file},
  # {GameLogger.start_map_load} and {Logger.upstream_stopped}; the typed
  # commands ({Logger.start_upstream_logger}); and $login_time, XMLData,
  # Script.current and Map.
  #
  # The helpers a run calls while it logs (the file, room and
  # typed-command methods below) are this class's own methods, called on
  # the run's class, so a later evaluation of log.lic, whatever its version,
  # cannot change them under a run already logging. The module methods a
  # run calls only while it starts ({GameLogger.timestamp_format},
  # {GameLogger.lines_per_file}, {GameLogger.exclude_entries},
  # {GameLogger.start_map_load}) stay on the module.
  #
  # The class is defined in the throwaway module +fresh+, after {Mode}
  # (whose XML line filter is this class's {StreamSpanFilter}), and set as
  # Logger below, once it is whole (see {Mode}); the name it is set to is
  # its name.
  class fresh::Logger
    # The line written after the reget lines at the top of the first file.
    REGET_MARKER = "<!-- Above contents from reget; full logging now active -->\n"

    # A run started less than this many seconds after login ($login_time)
    # writes reget's lines at the top of its first file.
    REGET_WINDOW = 30

    # XML mode's line filter ({Mode::XML}'s {Mode#line_filter}; one per
    # run): leaves out of each line the stream content lich-5 keeps out of
    # the main-window text it hands scripts, and keeps every other byte, so
    # the XML log holds what the main window shows, its markup included.
    # lich-5 builds that text with strip_xml (lib/common/markup.rb
    # SUPPRESSED_STREAMS and SPELLS_STREAM on lich-5 main,
    # lib/global_defs.rb strip_xml_simple at v5.21.0; the same patterns in
    # both). This filter models that span rule, tracked per line, and
    # leaves out:
    # - a span from a pushStream of the spellfront, inv, bounty, society,
    #   reserve, speech or talk stream to the next popStream, whatever stream
    #   that names, followed across lines;
    # - a <stream id="Spells"> element, within one line.
    #
    # It is a model, not an exact copy: lich-5 joins lines only while the
    # line's pushStreams outnumber its popStreams, so on a line that closes
    # one stream and then opens a left-out one ("<popStream id='combat'/>
    # <pushStream id='inv'/>...") lich-5 does not join, finds no popStream
    # after the push and shows the rest of that line and the lines after it,
    # while this filter leaves them out up to the next popStream. No line in
    # lich-5's stream fixtures has that form. The reserve id is in lich-5's
    # list from 5.19.0; on Lich 5.14.0 to 5.18.x Lich shows the reserve
    # stream, which this filter leaves out all the same.
    # Every other stream (combat, assess, percWindow, group, whispers,
    # atmospherics, familiar, room, experience, loot, ...) is main-window
    # text to lich-5 and is kept, its pushStream and popStream tags too. So
    # are prompts and the other tags lich-5 strips from its text but which
    # are not stream content.
    #
    # A line whose text is all kept is returned as it is, a blank one too; a
    # line that loses stream content and has nothing but whitespace left is
    # not written, as lich-5's strip drops a blank line. The two lines the
    # logger itself puts on the downstream buffer ({OWN_LINE}: a typed
    # command, which starts "<!-- CLIENT -->", and the upstream thread's
    # notice, "<!-- <name>: typed commands are not logged after this line; ")
    # are kept as they are, inside a span too, and are not read for stream
    # tags: a typed "<pushStream id='inv'/>" opens no span and a typed
    # "<popStream/>" closes none. Any other line, one that starts with
    # "<!-- " too, is a game line and is filtered like the rest.
    #
    # A line with a line break in it, which lich-5 never hands a script for a
    # game line (Script.new_downstream_xml chomps each one, and each is one
    # line the game sent), is one a script put on the downstream: lnet's chat
    # line, "<pushStream id=\"thoughts\"/>Bob: \"hi\"\n<popStream/>", is one.
    # When such a line comes inside a span, opens no left-out stream and
    # closes every stream it opens, it is kept whole and the span stays open:
    # the script wrote it to the client between two game lines, so it is not
    # span content, and its popStream closes its own stream, not the span.
    #
    # @note A stream that is opened and never closed leaves out every line
    #   after it until a popStream (of any stream) comes, as lich-5's own
    #   text does. In lich-5's stream fixtures every such stream is closed
    #   within 38 lines.
    # @note The span starts on the line the logger reads, so a run started
    #   inside one keeps its last lines and the popStream; the reget is
    #   written as it is and does not move the filter.
    class StreamSpanFilter
      # A pushStream that opens a span (lich-5's SUPPRESSED_STREAMS start).
      SPAN_START = %r{<pushStream id=["'](?:spellfront|inv|bounty|society|reserve|speech|talk)["'][^>]*/>}

      # A popStream, of any stream, which ends an open span.
      SPAN_END = /<popStream[^>]*>/

      # A Spells stream element (lich-5's SPELLS_STREAM).
      SPELLS = %r{<stream id="Spells">.*?</stream>}m

      # The start of any pushStream and of any popStream, counted to tell
      # whether a line closes every stream it opens.
      PUSH = '<pushStream'
      POP = '<popStream'

      # What is left of a line that is not written: nothing, or only
      # whitespace (lich-5's strip_simple drops such a line too).
      BLANK = /\A\s*\z/

      # How a line the logger itself put on the downstream buffer starts:
      # the {Mode::XML} upstream_line of a typed command, or the notice
      # {Logger.upstream_stopped} pushes (its name part is {Logger.log_label}).
      OWN_LINE = /\A<!-- (?:CLIENT -->|\S+: typed commands are not logged after this line; )/

      # The start every {OWN_LINE} shares, tested first so that most game
      # lines skip the regex.
      OWN_LINE_START = '<!-- '

      # A filter outside any span.
      #
      # @return [StreamSpanFilter]
      def initialize
        @in_span = false
      end

      # The text written for one game line, the lines given in the order the
      # run reads them; moves the filter into or out of a span.
      #
      # @param line [String] a game line as get returns it
      # @return [String, nil] +line+ itself when nothing of it is left out;
      #   the rest of it, as a binary String, when stream content is left
      #   out; +nil+ when that leaves nothing or only whitespace
      def call(line)
        return line if line.start_with?(OWN_LINE_START) && line.b.match?(OWN_LINE)
        return line unless @in_span || line.include?(PUSH) || line.include?('<stream id="Spells">')
        return line if @in_span && own_stream_line?(line.b)

        began_in_span = @in_span
        text = outside_spans(line.b).gsub(SPELLS, '')
        # An empty line inside a span is span content too.
        return line unless began_in_span || text.bytesize < line.bytesize

        # As Lich's strip does, a line left blank (empty, or only spaces) is not written.
        text.match?(BLANK) ? nil : text
      end

      private

      # Whether +bytes+ is a line a script put on the downstream with a stream
      # of its own: it has a line break in it (no game line has), opens no
      # left-out stream, and has as many popStreams as pushStreams, at least
      # one.
      #
      # @param bytes [String] a binary line
      # @return [Boolean]
      def own_stream_line?(bytes)
        return false unless bytes.include?("\n") && !bytes.match?(SPAN_START)

        pushes = bytes.scan(PUSH).size
        pushes.positive? && pushes == bytes.scan(POP).size
      end

      # The parts of +bytes+ outside the spans, from the span state the last
      # line left; leaves the state where this line ends.
      #
      # @param bytes [String] a binary game line
      # @return [String] the parts outside spans, joined
      def outside_spans(bytes)
        kept = String.new(encoding: Encoding::BINARY)
        position = 0
        loop do
          if @in_span
            close = SPAN_END.match(bytes, position)
            break unless close

            @in_span = false
            position = close.end(0)
          else
            open = SPAN_START.match(bytes, position)
            kept << bytes.byteslice(position, (open ? open.begin(0) : bytes.bytesize) - position)
            break unless open

            @in_span = true
            position = open.end(0)
          end
        end
        kept
      end
    end

    # The header and footer stamp when timestamps are off: the date, the
    # time to the millisecond and the UTC offset. A constant of this class
    # is safe from the session: each evaluation of log.lic builds a new
    # class, while a guarded constant on the module would keep the first
    # loaded value for the session.
    FILE_STAMP_FORMAT = '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S.%L %:z'

    # The script name that selects XML mode, kept for installs that still
    # have logxml.lic: it left the repository in log.lic 1.3.4, but neither
    # lich5-update nor the repository deletes a local script, and
    # logxml.lic 0.7.x loads this file, so a run started as ;logxml runs
    # under this name (see {mode_for}).
    XML_SCRIPT_NAME = 'logxml'

    # The name Lich gives the exec script --both starts for the XML log
    # ({GameLogger.start_xml_twin}), before its number (logxml1, logxml2,
    # ...). It differs from a local logxml.lic run's name, logxml, by that
    # number.
    BOTH_XML_NAME = 'logxml'

    # Picks the {Mode} for a run: XML mode when the options hold --xml (or
    # the word xml) or the script runs as {XML_SCRIPT_NAME}, with every line
    # kept ({XML_STREAMS_MODE}) when they also hold the word streams or
    # --streams, and text mode ({DEFAULT_MODE}) otherwise; text mode ignores
    # streams. The modes are this class's own, built with it, so a run never
    # gets a Mode from another evaluation of log.lic.
    #
    # @param options [OpenStruct] the run's options, as {Opts.parse} returns
    #   them; reads +xml+ and +streams+
    # @param script_name [String, nil] the name the script was started as
    #   (Script.current.name), e.g. "log", or "logxml" for an install's
    #   local logxml.lic 0.7.x
    # @return [Mode] {DEFAULT_MODE}, {XML_MODE} or {XML_STREAMS_MODE}
    def self.mode_for(options, script_name)
      return DEFAULT_MODE if options.both
      return DEFAULT_MODE unless options.xml || script_name == XML_SCRIPT_NAME

      options.streams ? XML_STREAMS_MODE : XML_MODE
    end

    # The name the running script was started as ("log", or "logxml" for
    # XML mode run by an install's local logxml.lic 0.7.x), which starts
    # the logger's own lines in the log file and in the Lich log, so they
    # name the logger to look at.
    #
    # @return [String] Script.current.name, or "log" when there is no
    #   current script
    def self.log_label
      Script.current&.name || 'log'
    end

    # Looks up the Lich room number for a game room UID in the map's UID
    # table. Map.ids_from_uid is a plain hash lookup: unlike Room.current and
    # Map.current it never matches the room by text, so it cannot send
    # LOCATION or PEER from the logger (which would clear the logger's own
    # buffer) and costs the same in rooms the map cannot place. It never
    # loads the map either (see {GameLogger.start_map_load}): until the map
    # is loaded, or learns the UID, every room has no Lich number here.
    #
    # @param uid [Integer] the game's room UID (XMLData.room_id)
    # @return [Integer, nil] the one Lich room number mapped to +uid+, or
    #   +nil+ when the UID maps to no room or to more than one
    def self.lich_room_id(uid)
      ids = Map.ids_from_uid(uid)
      ids.first if ids.size == 1
    end

    # Builds the matcher for the current room's title line, for --rnum. In
    # DragonRealms lich-5 wraps the bracketed title from the room subtitle in
    # one more pair of brackets (XMLData.room_title is "[[Title]]" while the
    # game line reads "[Title] (230008)"), so one pair is removed first.
    #
    # The pattern is built from the title's bytes, so match it against
    # +line.b+: a UTF-8 pattern with letters outside ASCII raises
    # Encoding::CompatibilityError on a binary game line with high bytes.
    #
    # @param title [String] XMLData.room_title
    # @param game [String] XMLData.game, e.g. "GSIV" or "DR"
    # @return [Regexp, nil] a pattern matching the bytes of a line that
    #   starts with the title, or +nil+ when the title is empty or not
    #   "[...]" shaped (before the first room title arrives), so no line is
    #   changed
    def self.room_title_pattern(title, game)
      title = title.to_s
      title = title[1..-2] if game.to_s.start_with?('DR') && title.start_with?('[[') && title.end_with?(']]')
      return nil unless title.length > 2 && title.start_with?('[') && title.end_with?(']')

      /\A#{Regexp.escape(title.b)}/
    end

    # Adds the room numbers to a room title line, for --rnum, in either
    # mode: " - <number>" inside the first "]" when the room has a Lich
    # number, and in GemStone " (u<uid>)" unless the line already shows
    # "] (<uid>)" (the game can send its room id after the title, as in
    # "[Sanctum of Scales, Columns] (4216015)", and a second one would say
    # the same twice). The u-id goes at the end of the line, or right after
    # the first "]" with +uid_after_title+ (XML mode, where logxml.lic
    # 0.6.6 put it). A room with no Lich number keeps its
    # title as the game sent it (apart from the GemStone UID). DragonRealms
    # titles never get a u-id, and neither does a room whose UID is unknown:
    # 0, or above 4294967296, which Lich's own room display shows as
    # "unknown" (lich-5 lib/games.rb).
    #
    # A line that shows another UID ("] (<number>)", read as Lich's own
    # room display reads it, lich-5 lib/games.rb modify_room_display) is
    # another room's title and is returned as it is. The logger reads
    # XMLData when it handles a line, not when the line came in, so after
    # quick moves the parser can already be a room ahead of the line, and
    # its numbers would be the next room's.
    #
    # @param line [String] a game line whose first "]" closes the room title
    # @param lich_id [Integer, nil] the room's Lich number, see {lich_room_id}
    # @param uid [Integer, nil] the game's room UID (XMLData.room_id)
    # @param game [String] XMLData.game, e.g. "GSIV" or "DR"
    # @param uid_after_title [Boolean] true puts the u-id right after the
    #   first "]", false (the default, text mode) at the end of the line; see
    #   {Mode#uid_after_title}
    # @return [String] a new String with the numbers added, or +line+ itself
    #   when there is nothing to add or the line shows another room's UID
    def self.number_room_title(line, lich_id, uid, game, uid_after_title: false)
      shown_uid = line.b[/\] \((\d+)\)/, 1]
      return line if shown_uid && shown_uid.to_i != uid.to_i

      line = line.sub(']') { " - #{lich_id}]" } if lich_id
      return line unless shown_uid.nil? && game.to_s.start_with?('GS') && uid.to_i.between?(1, 4_294_967_296)

      uid_after_title ? line.sub(']') { "] (u#{uid})" } : "#{line} (u#{uid})"
    end

    # The folder that holds every character's log folders: Lich's LOG_DIR,
    # which Lich's --logs= option sets (and which pilepull.lic reads these
    # logs from), or LICH_DIR/logs when LOG_DIR is not defined or empty.
    #
    # @return [String] the logs folder
    def self.log_root
      root = (LOG_DIR if defined?(LOG_DIR)).to_s
      root.empty? ? File.join(LICH_DIR, 'logs') : root
    end

    # The folder for one character's logs in one month:
    # <log root>/<game>-<name>/<YYYY>/<MM>, see {log_root}.
    #
    # @param game [String] XMLData.game, e.g. "GSIV" or "DR"
    # @param name [String] the character's name (XMLData.name)
    # @param time [Time] the time the log file is started
    # @return [String] the folder path (not created here)
    def self.log_directory(game, name, time)
      File.join(log_root, "#{game}-#{name}", time.strftime('%Y'), time.strftime('%m'))
    end

    # The path for a new log file: <dir>/<YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS><extension>.
    # The first file of a run keeps that name even when the file exists (it
    # is appended to, as a restarted logger always has). A file started by
    # rotation never reuses an existing name: when a whole file filled within
    # one second (a tiny --lines during a burst), or another logger wrote
    # that name, "_01", "_02", ... is added before the extension. That
    # suffix sorts after the first file's name and, up to _99, in order, so a
    # month folder listed by name keeps the files in the order written.
    #
    # @param dir [String] the folder, see {log_directory}
    # @param time [Time] the time the file is started
    # @param extension [String] the file extension with its dot, e.g. ".log"
    # @param rotated [Boolean] true for a file started by rotation (a new day
    #   or the --lines count), false for the first file of a run
    # @return [String] the file path
    def self.log_file_path(dir, time, extension, rotated:)
      stem = File.join(dir, time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S'))
      path = "#{stem}#{extension}"
      return path unless rotated

      suffix = 0
      path = "#{stem}_#{format('%02d', suffix += 1)}#{extension}" while File.exist?(path)
      path
    end

    # A log file name this logger writes: <YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS>, an optional
    # rotation suffix (_01), then .log or .xml. The date in the name, not the
    # file's modification time, decides its age (copying logs between
    # machines resets modification times).
    LOG_FILE_NAME = /\A(\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})_\d{2}-\d{2}-\d{2}(?:_\d+)?\.(?:log|xml)\z/

    # Seconds a new day's sweep waits before it starts (see
    # {#start_compression}).
    DAY_SWEEP_DELAY = 120

    # Seconds since its last write under which a file dated yesterday is
    # left alone, and after which a leftover .gz.tmp counts as abandoned. A
    # file a logger in this Lich process holds open is skipped whatever its
    # age ({GameLogger.log_path_open?}); this guard is the backstop for a
    # logger that does not record its files, such as a logxml.lic older
    # than 0.7.
    RECENT_WRITE = 3600

    # One character's log folder: <log root>/<game>-<name>, see {log_root}.
    #
    # @param game [String] XMLData.game, e.g. "GSIV" or "DR"
    # @param name [String] the character's name (XMLData.name)
    # @return [String] the folder path
    def self.character_log_folder(game, name)
      File.join(log_root, "#{game}-#{name}")
    end

    # Gzips every log file in a character's month folders
    # (<folder>/<YYYY>/<MM>/) whose name dates it before +cutoff+, one file
    # at a time (see {compress_log_file}). Files with other names, .gz
    # files, the folder root (the layout before log.lic 0.0.7) and any file
    # a logger in this Lich process holds open ({GameLogger.log_path_open?})
    # are left alone.
    #
    # @param folder [String] the character's log folder, see
    #   {character_log_folder}
    # @param cutoff [Date] files dated before this day are compressed
    # @param now [Time] the time now, for {RECENT_WRITE}
    # @return [Array(Integer, Integer, Integer)] the files compressed, their
    #   bytes before and their bytes after
    def self.compress_old_logs(folder, cutoff, now: Time.now)
      count = before = after = 0
      # base: keeps glob characters in LOG_DIR ([, {, \\ on Windows) out of
      # the pattern.
      Dir.glob('[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]/[0-9][0-9]/*', base: folder).sort.each do |relative|
        path = File.join(folder, relative)
        # A temp file whose log is gone (another sweep finished it, or it
        # was moved away) is removed once abandoned; one beside its log is
        # handled with the log, in compress_log_file.
        if relative.end_with?('.gz.tmp')
          remove_abandoned_temp(path, now) unless File.exist?(path.delete_suffix('.gz.tmp'))
          next
        end
        next unless (match = File.basename(path).match(LOG_FILE_NAME))
        next unless (date = (Date.new(match[1].to_i, match[2].to_i, match[3].to_i) rescue nil))&.<(cutoff)
        # A file a logger in this process holds open is never compressed: an
        # idle logger keeps yesterday's file open until a game line comes,
        # and on Linux and macOS deleting it would lose its later lines.
        next if GameLogger.log_path_open?(path)
        # Only a file dated yesterday (or later) can still be in use; an
        # older one is compressed even if just copied in (copying resets the
        # modification time, not the name).
        next unless (sizes = compress_log_file(path, now: now, recent_guard: date >= now.to_date - 1))

        count += 1
        before += sizes[0]
        after += sizes[1]
      end
      [count, before, after]
    end

    # Gzips one log file in place, as <path>.gz, without ever losing it:
    # the copy is written to <path>.gz.tmp (created exclusively, so a second
    # logger compressing the same folder skips the file), flushed to disk,
    # read back whole and checked against the original's size, then renamed
    # to <path>.gz, but only while that temp file is still the one this call
    # wrote, and given the original's modification time and permissions;
    # only then is the original deleted. A file written to within
    # {RECENT_WRITE} is skipped. When <path>.gz already exists (an earlier
    # sweep stopped after the rename), the original is deleted only if that
    # .gz holds exactly its bytes ({finish_compressed}). A file another
    # sweeper finished meanwhile is left to it. Every failure leaves the
    # original in place, removes the temp file this call made, and goes to
    # the Lich log with the path and the error.
    #
    # @param path [String] the log file
    # @param now [Time] the time now, for {RECENT_WRITE}
    # @param recent_guard [Boolean] skip the file when it was written to
    #   within {RECENT_WRITE} ({compress_old_logs} turns this off for files
    #   dated before yesterday)
    # @return [Array(Integer, Integer), nil] the original's size and the
    #   .gz's size when the original is gone, or +nil+ when it is still there
    def self.compress_log_file(path, now: Time.now, recent_guard: true)
      stat = File.stat(path)
      return nil if recent_guard && now - stat.mtime < RECENT_WRITE

      gz_path = "#{path}.gz"
      tmp = "#{gz_path}.tmp"
      remove_abandoned_temp(tmp, now)
      return finish_compressed(path, gz_path, stat) if File.exist?(gz_path)

      created = false
      written = nil
      File.open(tmp, File::WRONLY | File::CREAT | File::EXCL | File::BINARY) do |out|
        created = true
        gzip = Zlib::GzipWriter.new(out, Zlib::DEFAULT_COMPRESSION)
        gzip.mtime = stat.mtime
        gzip.orig_name = File.basename(path)
        File.open(path, 'rb') do |input|
          while (chunk = input.read(65_536))
            gzip.write(chunk)
          end
        end
        gzip.finish
        out.flush
        out.fsync
        written = out.stat
      end
      unless gzip_complete?(tmp, stat.size) && File.size(path) == stat.size
        File.delete(tmp)
        created = false
        Lich.log "log: left #{path} as it is: its compressed copy did not read back whole, or the file changed meanwhile"
        return nil
      end
      unless same_file?(File.stat(tmp), written)
        created = false
        Lich.log "log: left #{path} as it is: another sweep replaced its compressed copy before it was renamed"
        return nil
      end
      File.rename(tmp, gz_path)
      created = false
      sync_directory(File.dirname(gz_path))
      keep_times_and_mode(gz_path, stat)
      remove_compressed_original(path, gz_path, stat)
    rescue Errno::EEXIST
      # Another sweeper is compressing this file.
      nil
    rescue Errno::ENOENT
      # Another sweeper finished this file meanwhile; drop this call's copy.
      remove_own_temp(tmp, created)
      nil
    rescue SystemCallError, IOError, Zlib::Error => e
      remove_own_temp(tmp, created)
      Lich.log "log: left #{path} as it is: compressing it failed (#{e.class}: #{e.message})"
      nil
    end

    # Deletes the temp file a {compress_log_file} call made, when it made
    # one and it is still there.
    #
    # @param tmp [String, nil] the temp file
    # @param created [Boolean] whether this call created it
    # @return [void]
    def self.remove_own_temp(tmp, created)
      File.delete(tmp) if created && tmp && File.exist?(tmp)
    rescue SystemCallError
      nil
    end

    # Deletes a temp file left over an hour ago ({RECENT_WRITE}): a sweep
    # that was stopped, or one whose log another sweep finished. A younger
    # one may belong to a sweep still running and is left alone.
    #
    # @param tmp [String] the temp file
    # @param now [Time] the time now
    # @return [void]
    def self.remove_abandoned_temp(tmp, now)
      File.delete(tmp) if File.exist?(tmp) && now - File.mtime(tmp) > RECENT_WRITE
    rescue SystemCallError
      nil
    end

    # Whether two stats are of the same file: size and modification time,
    # and the inode where the system has one (Windows reports 0).
    #
    # @param now [File::Stat] the file's stat now
    # @param written [File::Stat, nil] its stat right after it was written
    # @return [Boolean]
    def self.same_file?(now, written)
      return false if written.nil?
      return false unless now.size == written.size && now.mtime == written.mtime

      now.ino.zero? || written.ino.zero? || now.ino == written.ino
    end

    # Flushes a rename in +dir+ to disk where the system allows it (not on
    # Windows, where a folder cannot be opened), so a crash right after
    # cannot keep the original's deletion and lose the .gz's rename.
    #
    # @param dir [String] the folder
    # @return [void]
    def self.sync_directory(dir)
      File.open(dir, &:fsync)
    rescue SystemCallError, IOError
      nil
    end

    # Gives the .gz the original's modification time and permissions. Both
    # are best effort: some network mounts refuse them, and the .gz is
    # whole either way.
    #
    # @param gz_path [String] the .gz
    # @param stat [File::Stat] the original's stat
    # @return [void]
    def self.keep_times_and_mode(gz_path, stat)
      File.utime(stat.atime, stat.mtime, gz_path)
      File.chmod(stat.mode & 0o777, gz_path)
    rescue SystemCallError => e
      Lich.log "log: compressed #{gz_path}, but could not give it the original's time or permissions (#{e.class}: #{e.message})"
    end

    # Deletes the original once <path>.gz holds it (see
    # {compress_log_file}). When it cannot be deleted (on Windows, a file
    # another program has open), both stay, and the next sweep deletes the
    # original after checking the .gz ({finish_compressed}). An original
    # another sweeper already deleted counts as done.
    #
    # @param path [String] the log file
    # @param gz_path [String] its .gz
    # @param stat [File::Stat] the log file's stat from before compressing
    # @return [Array(Integer, Integer), nil] the sizes, or +nil+ when the
    #   original is still there
    def self.remove_compressed_original(path, gz_path, stat)
      begin
        File.delete(path)
      rescue Errno::ENOENT
        nil
      end
      [stat.size, File.size(gz_path)]
    rescue SystemCallError => e
      Lich.log "log: compressed #{path} to #{gz_path}, but could not delete the original (#{e.class}: #{e.message}); the next sweep deletes it"
      nil
    end

    # Finishes a file an earlier sweep compressed but did not delete: the
    # original goes only when <path>.gz holds exactly its bytes (same size
    # and CRC-32, see {gzip_matches?}), after the .gz is given the
    # original's time and permissions; otherwise both stay and the Lich log
    # says why.
    #
    # @param path [String] the log file
    # @param gz_path [String] its existing .gz
    # @param stat [File::Stat] the log file's stat
    # @return [Array(Integer, Integer), nil] the sizes when the original is
    #   gone, or +nil+ when it is still there
    def self.finish_compressed(path, gz_path, stat)
      unless gzip_matches?(gz_path, path)
        Lich.log "log: left #{path} as it is: #{gz_path} already exists and does not hold exactly its contents"
        return nil
      end
      keep_times_and_mode(gz_path, stat)
      remove_compressed_original(path, gz_path, stat)
    end

    # Whether +gz_path+ is a whole gzip file holding +size+ bytes: read to
    # the end, which also checks gzip's CRC and length trailer.
    #
    # @param gz_path [String] the .gz (or .gz.tmp) file
    # @param size [Integer] the original's size in bytes
    # @return [Boolean]
    def self.gzip_complete?(gz_path, size)
      gzip_digest(gz_path)&.last == size
    end

    # Whether +gz_path+ holds exactly the bytes of +path+: the same size
    # and CRC-32 after reading both to the end.
    #
    # @param gz_path [String] the .gz
    # @param path [String] the original
    # @return [Boolean]
    def self.gzip_matches?(gz_path, path)
      digest = gzip_digest(gz_path)
      return false if digest.nil?

      crc = 0
      size = 0
      File.open(path, 'rb') do |input|
        while (chunk = input.read(65_536))
          crc = Zlib.crc32(chunk, crc)
          size += chunk.bytesize
        end
      end
      digest == [crc, size]
    rescue SystemCallError, IOError
      false
    end

    # The CRC-32 and byte count of everything +gz_path+ decompresses to, or
    # +nil+ when it is not a whole gzip file (gzip's own CRC and length
    # trailer are checked on the way).
    #
    # @param gz_path [String] the .gz (or .gz.tmp) file
    # @return [Array(Integer, Integer), nil] [crc, size]
    def self.gzip_digest(gz_path)
      crc = 0
      size = 0
      Zlib::GzipReader.open(gz_path) do |gzip|
        while (chunk = gzip.read(65_536))
          crc = Zlib.crc32(chunk, crc)
          size += chunk.bytesize
        end
      end
      [crc, size]
    rescue Zlib::Error, IOError, SystemCallError
      nil
    end

    # A byte count for the echo: "438 MB", "4.9 GB".
    #
    # @param bytes [Integer]
    # @return [String]
    def self.size_text(bytes)
      return "#{(bytes / 1e9).round(1)} GB" if bytes >= 1e9
      return "#{(bytes / 1e6).round} MB" if bytes >= 1e6

      "#{(bytes / 1e3).round} KB"
    end

    # The header or footer line of a log file: the time now in the
    # timestamp format, or in {FILE_STAMP_FORMAT} (date, time to the
    # millisecond and UTC offset) when timestamps are off.
    #
    # @param stamp_format [String, nil] the strftime format, or +nil+ for no
    #   timestamps
    # @param clock [#now] where the time comes from; Time by default (a spec
    #   can pass a fixed clock)
    # @return [String] the line, without a line end
    def self.file_stamp(stamp_format, clock: Time)
      clock.now.strftime(stamp_format || FILE_STAMP_FORMAT)
    end

    # Opens a log file for appending, writes its header line, runs the block
    # with it, then writes its footer line and closes it. The footer is
    # written exactly once, from an ensure, so a file gets one also when the
    # logger stops (;kill, logout, Lich exiting) and not only when it starts a
    # new file; see {write_footer}. A file whose header could not be written
    # gets no footer, and is still closed, so an error in the header never
    # leaves the file open. A close that fails goes to the Lich log with the
    # file's path and the error, and is otherwise ignored. Lines are written
    # as they come (sync is on), but a close that fails can still mean a
    # missing footer or a write error the system reported late.
    #
    # @param filename [String] the log file path; an existing file is
    #   appended to
    # @param stamp_format [String, nil] the strftime format for the header and
    #   footer, or +nil+ for the default, see {file_stamp}
    # @param clock [#now] where the header and footer times come from; Time
    #   by default
    # @param header_stamp [Proc, nil] used for the header line instead of
    #   {file_stamp}'s default when +stamp_format+ is +nil+: called with the
    #   time, returns the line (see {Mode#header_stamp}); +nil+ uses the
    #   default, as the footer always does
    # @yield [file] writes the file's lines
    # @yieldparam file [File] the open log file, with sync on
    # @yieldreturn [void]
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemCallError, IOError] when the file cannot be opened, its
    #   header cannot be written, or the block returns (the file is full or
    #   the day changed) and the footer cannot be written; the file is closed
    #   first. Errors raised by the block pass through after the footer and
    #   close.
    def self.with_log_file(filename, stamp_format, clock: Time, header_stamp: nil)
      # Recorded before the open, so no sweep starts on it in between.
      open_key = GameLogger.open_log_path(filename)
      begin
        file = File.open(filename, 'a')
      rescue StandardError
        GameLogger.close_log_path(open_key)
        raise
      end
      header_written = false
      finished = false
      begin
        file.sync = true
        file.puts(stamp_format.nil? && header_stamp ? header_stamp.call(clock.now) : file_stamp(stamp_format, clock: clock))
        header_written = true
        yield file
        finished = true
      ensure
        begin
          # Not finished: an error or Thread#kill (;kill) is stopping the logger.
          write_footer(file, stamp_format, stopping: !finished, clock: clock) if header_written
        ensure
          begin
            file.close
          rescue StandardError => e
            Lich.log "#{log_label}: closing the log file #{filename} failed (#{e.class}: #{e.message}); lines are written as they come, with sync on, but the file may be missing its footer or a last write"
          ensure
            GameLogger.close_log_path(open_key)
          end
        end
      end
    end

    # Writes a log file's footer line. It runs each time a file is closed:
    # when a new file starts (a new day or the --lines count), where a failed
    # write raises, and when the logger stops, where a failed write (disk
    # full, a closed file) goes to the Lich log and is otherwise ignored, so
    # it does not replace the reason the logger stopped.
    #
    # @param file [File] the open log file
    # @param stamp_format [String, nil] the strftime format, or +nil+ for
    #   the default, see {file_stamp}
    # @param stopping [Boolean] true when the logger is stopping (an error or
    #   ;kill), false when the file is closed for the next one
    # @param clock [#now] where the footer time comes from; Time by default
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [IOError, SystemCallError] when the write fails and +stopping+ is
    #   false
    def self.write_footer(file, stamp_format, stopping:, clock: Time)
      file.puts file_stamp(stamp_format, clock: clock)
    rescue IOError, SystemCallError => e
      raise unless stopping

      Lich.log "#{log_label}: the footer of #{file.path} was not written while the logger stopped (#{e.class}: #{e.message})"
      nil
    end

    # Starts the thread that logs typed commands: each command lich-5 hands
    # the script's upstream buffer is pushed onto its downstream buffer as
    # the block formats it, by default as ">command" (a leading "<c>"
    # removed, the {Mode#upstream_line} of {DEFAULT_MODE}), so the get loop
    # in {#run} writes it in order with the game lines. The format comes
    # as a block, not an argument, so a wrapper that calls +super+ with no
    # arguments passes it on. When the thread stops on an error, it
    # says why and leaves a notice in the log file, see {upstream_stopped}.
    # Thread#kill (;kill, logout) stops it without either, as does an
    # execution-guard stop (see {guard_stop?}), and so does a
    # StopIteration from upstream_get or the push: Kernel#loop rescues that
    # one itself and ends the loop. lich-5's upstream_get does not raise it.
    #
    # @note A reply the game sends within about 50 ms of a typed command
    #   can be logged before the command's line. lich-5 hands this thread
    #   the command by polling the upstream buffer every 50 ms
    #   (Script#upstream_gets, lib/common/script.rb), while the get loop
    #   wakes at once for a game line (Script#gets, a blocking wait_shift),
    #   so a fast reply can reach the downstream buffer first. The longer
    #   the round trip to the game, the rarer it is. ;log help says so
    #   too; a fix belongs in lich-5.
    # @yield [command] formats one typed command for the log; with no
    #   block, {DEFAULT_MODE}'s {Mode#upstream_line} is used
    # @yieldparam command [String] the command as upstream_get returns it
    # @yieldreturn [String] the line to log
    # @return [Thread] the started thread
    def self.start_upstream_logger(&format_command)
      format_command ||= DEFAULT_MODE.upstream_line
      Thread.new do
        loop { Script.current.downstream_buffer.push format_command.call(upstream_get) }
      rescue StandardError => e
        upstream_stopped(e) unless guard_stop?(e)
      end
    end

    # Whether +error+ is lich-5's execution-guard stop
    # (Lich::Common::ScriptExecutionGuard::Interrupted, lich-5 5.22 and
    # later), which a guarded script's threads get instead of Thread#kill.
    # It ends the upstream thread as quietly as ;kill does: no echo, no
    # notice line. Before 5.22 the class does not exist and nothing is a
    # guard stop.
    #
    # @param error [StandardError] the error that stopped the upstream thread
    # @return [Boolean]
    def self.guard_stop?(error)
      return false unless defined?(::Lich::Common::ScriptExecutionGuard::Interrupted)

      error.is_a?(::Lich::Common::ScriptExecutionGuard::Interrupted)
    end

    # Says that typed commands are no longer logged because the upstream
    # thread (see {start_upstream_logger}) stopped on +error+: echoes the
    # error's class and message, the file and line it was raised at (no
    # directory, see {upstream_frame}) and how to restart the logger with the
    # arguments it was started with, and pushes one comment line onto the
    # downstream buffer, so the log file shows where the typed command lines
    # end. The comment starts with the name the logger runs as (see
    # {log_label}). The game lines are still logged. That line goes through
    # the get loop like a game line: it is stamped when timestamps are on,
    # and it is not written while the logger is in an --exclude room.
    #
    # Both modes set want_script_output, so in lich-5 the echo reaches the
    # downstream buffer too and is logged just before the notice line.
    #
    # @param error [StandardError] the error that stopped the thread
    # @return [void]
    def self.upstream_stopped(error)
      reason = "#{error.class}: #{error.message.to_s.gsub(/\s+/, ' ').strip}"
      where = upstream_frame(error)
      script = Script.current
      echo "typed commands are no longer logged: the upstream thread stopped on #{reason}#{" at #{where}" if where}. " \
           "Game lines are still logged; restart the logger (#{restart_hint(script)}) to log typed commands again."
      # A "-" before another "-" is spaced out: "--" would end the comment
      # early, and is not allowed inside an XML comment. The ")" after the
      # reason keeps a last "-" off the closing "-->".
      script.downstream_buffer.push "<!-- #{log_label}: typed commands are not logged after this line; the upstream thread stopped (#{reason.gsub(/-(?=-)/, '- ')}) -->"
    end

    # How to restart +script+ with the arguments it was started with:
    # ";kill log, then ;log --xml". When another script of the same name
    # runs too (;log plus ;force log --xml, see {help_lines}), Lich refuses a start without ;force while either runs, so
    # every restart uses ;force. Lich's ;kill log stops the first script
    # named exactly log in its running list, then its hidden list, both in
    # start order (both loggers call hide_me): the log started first. So
    # for the logger started first the hint is one ;kill and one ;force;
    # for a later one, ;kill log stops the earlier logs before this one, so
    # the hint stops them all and starts them again in the same order, each
    # with the arguments it was started with. The XML twin of --both
    # (named {BOTH_XML_NAME} and a number) is started by its text logger,
    # so its hint restarts that one with --both.
    #
    # @param script [Script] the running logger (Script.current)
    # @return [String] the restart commands, without parentheses
    def self.restart_hint(script)
      name = script.name.to_s
      return ';kill log, then start ;log --both again (this XML log is its --both twin)' if name.match?(/\A#{BOTH_XML_NAME}\d+\z/)

      peers = same_name_scripts(script)
      return ";kill #{name}, then #{start_command(name, script, force: false)}" if peers.empty?

      order = kill_order(name)
      killed = order.include?(script) ? order[0..order.index { |other| other.equal?(script) }] : [script]
      if killed.size == 1
        return ";kill #{name}, then #{start_command(name, script, force: true)}; " \
               "another #{name} is running too, and ;kill #{name} stops the one started first, which is this one"
      end

      # The first restart needs ;force only when a script of that name is
      # still running after the kills.
      force_first = peers.any? { |other| killed.none? { |gone| gone.equal?(other) } }
      starts = killed.each_with_index.map { |other, index| start_command(name, other, force: force_first || index.positive?) }
      times = killed.size == 2 ? 'twice' : "#{killed.size} times"
      earlier = killed.size == 2 ? "the #{name} started before this one" : "the #{killed.size - 1} #{name}s started before this one"
      ";kill #{name} #{times}, one at a time, then #{starts.join(', then ')}; " \
        ";kill #{name} stops #{earlier} first, so this stops #{killed.size == 2 ? 'both' : 'all of them'} and starts them again in that order"
    end

    # The start command for +script+ under +name+, with the arguments it
    # was started with: ";log --rnum", or ";force log --rnum" with +force+.
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the loggers run as
    # @param script [Script] the logger to start again
    # @param force [Boolean] whether the start needs ;force
    # @return [String]
    def self.start_command(name, script, force:)
      args = script.vars&.first.to_s.strip
      [";#{'force ' if force}#{name}", args].reject(&:empty?).join(' ')
    end

    # The scripts named exactly +name+ in the order Lich's ;kill +name+
    # stops them, one per ;kill: the running list, then the hidden list,
    # each in start order (lich-5 lib/common/client_commands/builtins.rb).
    #
    # @param name [String] the name the loggers run as
    # @return [Array<Script>]
    def self.kill_order(name)
      (Script.running + Script.hidden).select { |other| other.name.to_s == name }.uniq(&:object_id)
    end

    # The scripts other than +script+ that run under its name (as Lich's
    # start refuses a duplicate: any case), hidden or not; both loggers
    # call hide_me.
    #
    # @param script [Script] the running logger (Script.current)
    # @return [Array<Script>]
    def self.same_name_scripts(script)
      (Script.running + Script.hidden).select { |other| !other.equal?(script) && other.name.to_s.casecmp?(script.name.to_s) }
    end

    # The file name and line +error+ was raised at, from its first backtrace
    # frame without the directory, which keeps the echo short. (The log file
    # still shows the full log path, in the "currently logging to" echo.)
    #
    # @param error [StandardError] the error the upstream thread stopped on
    # @return [String, nil] "file:line", the frame as is when it has no
    #   "path:line" start, or nil when the error has no backtrace
    def self.upstream_frame(error)
      frame = error.backtrace&.first
      return nil if frame.nil?

      match = frame.match(/\A(.+?):(\d+)/)
      match ? "#{File.basename(match[1])}:#{match[2]}" : frame
    end

    # Sets up a run; nothing is read, set or started until {#run}.
    #
    # @param options [OpenStruct] the run's options, as {Opts.parse} returns
    #   them; the run reads these (`:xml` and `:streams` are read by
    #   {Logger.mode_for}, which {GameLogger.main} uses to pick +mode+; this
    #   method does not read them):
    # @option options [String, true, nil] :timestamp --timestamp=FMT, the
    #   strftime format for line stamps (see {GameLogger.timestamp_format})
    # @option options [String, true, nil] :rnum --rnum, or the word rnum:
    #   any value numbers room titles
    # @option options [String, true, nil] :roomnum --roomnum, the same as
    #   +:rnum+
    # @option options [String, Array<String>, true, nil] :exclude
    #   --exclude=u7199,288, the rooms whose lines are not written (see
    #   {GameLogger.exclude_entries})
    # @option options [String, true, nil] :lines --lines=N, the lines
    #   written per file (see {GameLogger.lines_per_file})
    # @param mode [Mode] the log format; by default {DEFAULT_MODE}, the
    #   {Mode::TEXT} built with this class
    # @param clock [#now] where every time the run uses comes from (file
    #   names and folders, the new-day check, line stamps, the reget window,
    #   the header and footer); Time by default
    # @param io [Object] answers Lich's script helpers +get+ (every game
    #   line), +reget+, +echo+ (the start echo only) and, when it has one,
    #   +get_settings+. They are called with send, as Lich defines them as
    #   private top-level methods. GameLogger by default, which reaches
    #   them in Lich (and which the spec harness gives stand-ins). The
    #   other echoes still go to Lich's echo, and typed commands do not
    #   come through +io+: {Logger.start_upstream_logger} reads
    #   Lich's upstream_get and pushes each command into
    #   Script.current.downstream_buffer, which Lich's get reads, so with
    #   an +io+ whose +get+ reads elsewhere typed commands are not logged.
    def initialize(options, mode: DEFAULT_MODE, clock: Time, io: GameLogger)
      @options = options
      @mode = mode
      @clock = clock
      @io = io
      @line_filter = mode.line_filter.call
    end

    # Runs the logger until the script is killed: starts a new log file on
    # each new day, with the first line read after midnight (excluded or
    # not), and after the --lines count of written lines (see
    # {GameLogger.lines_per_file}; excluded and filtered lines do not
    # count), when the next line is read, so the next file is named and
    # dated by its first line; stamps lines when
    # {GameLogger.timestamp_format} returns a format. The file header and
    # footer carry that format, or the date and time when there is none; a
    # file also gets its footer when the logger stops (see
    # {Logger.with_log_file}). Typed commands are logged as the mode's
    # {Mode#upstream_line} makes them (see
    # {Logger.start_upstream_logger}), lines seen in --exclude rooms
    # are skipped, and --rnum adds the room number to room titles.
    #
    # The room for --exclude and --rnum is read from XMLData and the map's
    # UID table (see {Logger.lich_room_id}), never from Room.current or
    # Map.current. When a Lich number is needed, the map is loaded once in
    # the background (see {GameLogger.start_map_load}). A found Lich number
    # is kept until the room changes; while there is none, or while that
    # load is still running, each line asks the map again, so a room is
    # numbered and excluded from the line after the map learns its UID.
    #
    # @note A line is checked against the room the parser holds when the
    #   logger reads the line, not when the game sent it. When the logger
    #   falls behind a room change, a room title can be left without numbers
    #   or get the next room's numbers, and lines at the edge of an excluded
    #   room can be skipped or logged on the wrong side.
    # @note Lines read before the map has loaded are logged even in a room
    #   excluded by Lich number (a u<uid> entry needs no map).
    # @note When another script reloads the map, the UID table is cleared
    #   and rebuilt one room at a time. A UID shared by several rooms can
    #   then show just one of them, and the logger keeps that Lich number
    #   for the rest of the room. Only the logger's own load is waited out.
    # @note Lich evaluates `module GameLogger` again for every run in the
    #   session (;force log). That replaces +Logger+ and {Mode} (see
    #   {Mode}), and a run already logging keeps its own class, with the
    #   file, room and typed-command helpers it calls while it logs (such as
    #   {Logger.log_file_path} and {Logger.with_log_file}). What it reads
    #   only while it starts is still shared: the module methods each later
    #   run redefines ({GameLogger.timestamp_format},
    #   {GameLogger.lines_per_file}, {GameLogger.exclude_entries},
    #   {GameLogger.start_map_load}, {Opts}) and the module constants set
    #   only when not yet defined ({GameLogger::DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE},
    #   {GameLogger::DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT},
    #   {GameLogger::LINE_COUNT_PATTERN}, {Opts::FLAG_PREFIX},
    #   {Opts::LIST_FLAGS}), which keep the first run's values for the
    #   session.
    # @note A reply that comes within about 50 ms of a typed command can be
    #   logged before the command's line; see {Logger.start_upstream_logger}.
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemCallError, IOError] when the month's log folder cannot
    #   be created, a log file cannot be opened, or a write to it fails (the
    #   file is closed first; see {Logger.with_log_file})
    def run
      configure
      self.class.start_upstream_logger(&@mode.upstream_line)
      @started = false
      # The line that opens the next file. A line read after midnight belongs
      # to the new day's file: it is kept here while the old file is closed.
      # After a full file, the next line is read before the next file is
      # opened, so its name and date are those of its first line: a file
      # opened at once would wait with no line, and when that line came after
      # midnight it would go to the new day's file, leaving an old-day file
      # with only its header and footer.
      @carried_line = nil
      loop { log_next_file }
    end

    # The text written for a game line, by the run's line filter (built
    # from the mode's {Mode#line_filter} with the run): text mode drops a
    # line that starts with a stream, dialog or progress bar tag; XML mode
    # leaves out stream content and keeps the rest ({StreamSpanFilter}),
    # and with streams keeps every line. Room exclusion is checked
    # separately. XML mode's filter follows stream spans from line to line,
    # so the run gives it every line it reads, in order, those in an
    # excluded room too.
    #
    # @param line [String] a game line as get returns it
    # @return [String, nil] the text to write (`line` itself when the filter
    #   keeps all of it), or +nil+ when nothing of the line is written
    def line_text(line)
      @line_filter.call(line)
    end

    # Adds the room numbers to +line+ when --rnum is on and the mode says it
    # is the current room's title line ({Mode#room_title}), with
    # {Logger.number_room_title}, the decorator both modes share, placing
    # the GemStone u-id where the mode's {Mode#uid_after_title} says. With
    # --rnum off no line is numbered, also when --exclude tracks the room.
    #
    # @param line [String] a game line
    # @return [String] the line with the room numbers added, or +line+
    #   itself when it is not the title line (or --rnum is off)
    def decorate_title(line)
      return line unless @show_room_numbers && @mode.room_title.call(line, @title_pattern)

      self.class.number_room_title(line, @lich_id, @room_uid, XMLData.game, uid_after_title: @mode.uid_after_title)
    end

    # The text written to the file for one line: "<time>: <line>" in the
    # run's timestamp format, or the line as it is when timestamps are off.
    #
    # @param line [String] the line to write
    # @param time [Time] the time to stamp it with
    # @return [String] the text, without a line end
    def format_line(line, time)
      @stamp_format ? "#{time.strftime(@stamp_format)}: #{line}" : line
    end

    # reget's answer as the lines written at the top of the first file,
    # each formatted by the mode's {Mode#reget_line}.
    #
    # @param lines [Array<String>, nil] reget's answer; +nil+ (an empty
    #   buffer) gives no lines
    # @param login_time [Time] when the character logged in ($login_time)
    # @return [Array<String>] the lines to write, without line ends
    def reget_lines(lines, login_time)
      Array(lines).map { |line| @mode.reget_line.call(line, @stamp_format, login_time) }
    end

    private

    # Reads the settings and options and sets the script's flags, in the
    # order the logger always has: get_settings, the mode's
    # {Mode#script_flags}, then the timestamp format, the --lines count and
    # the --compress-after days (each may echo), then the map load when a
    # Lich number is needed. Every value goes on this run.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def configure
      settings = nil
      if @io.respond_to?(:get_settings, true)
        settings = @io.send(:get_settings)
        stamp_enable = settings.log_timestamp
        stamp_format = settings.log_timestamp_format
      end
      @mode.script_flags.each { |flag, value| Script.current.public_send(:"#{flag}=", value) }
      @stamp_format = GameLogger.timestamp_format(@options.timestamp, stamp_enable, stamp_format)
      @show_room_numbers = @options.roomnum || @options.rnum
      @lines_per_file = GameLogger.lines_per_file(@options.lines)
      # The XML twin of --both shares this character folder; only the text
      # logger compresses it.
      @compress_days = (GameLogger.compress_after_days(@options[:'compress-after'], settings&.log_compress_after) unless @options[GameLogger::TWIN_KEY])
      @compress_date = @compressor = nil
      configure_rooms
    end

    # Works out which room lookups the run needs from --exclude and --rnum,
    # starts the map load when a Lich number is needed, and clears the
    # room state, so the first line works the room out.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def configure_rooms
      @uid_excludes, @id_excludes = GameLogger.exclude_entries(@options.exclude)
      @look_up_lich_id = @show_room_numbers || !@id_excludes.empty?
      @track_room = @look_up_lich_id || !@uid_excludes.empty?
      @map_loader = (GameLogger.start_map_load if @look_up_lich_id)
      @room_count = @room_uid = @room_title = @title_pattern = @lich_id = nil
      @room_uid_excluded = @room_id_excluded = false
    end

    # Opens the next log file (in the month's folder, created when
    # missing), writes the reget and the start echo when it is the run's
    # first file, and fills it with lines until it is full or the day
    # changes. The first file of a run takes its name from the time it
    # starts; every later one is opened when its first line has been read.
    #
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemCallError, IOError] when the month's folder cannot be
    #   created, and as {Logger.with_log_file} raises
    def log_next_file
      @carried_line = read_line if @started && @carried_line.nil?
      dir = self.class.log_directory(XMLData.game, XMLData.name, @clock.now)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(dir) unless File.exist?(dir)
      date = today
      start_compression(date) if @compress_days && date != @compress_date
      filename = self.class.log_file_path(dir, @clock.now, @mode.extension, rotated: @started)
      self.class.with_log_file(filename, @stamp_format, clock: @clock, header_stamp: @mode.header_stamp) do |file|
        start_logging(file, filename) unless @started
        fill_file(file, date)
      end
    end

    # Writes reget's lines and the reget marker at the top of the run's
    # first file when the run started within {REGET_WINDOW} seconds of
    # login, then echoes where the log goes.
    #
    # @param file [File] the run's first log file
    # @param filename [String] its path, for the echo
    # @return [void]
    def start_logging(file, filename)
      if (@clock.now - $login_time) < REGET_WINDOW
        reget_lines(@io.send(:reget), $login_time).each { |line| file.puts line }
        file.puts REGET_MARKER
      end

      @io.send(:echo, "#{@mode.started_text} #{filename}")
      @started = true
    end

    # Writes lines to +file+ until --lines lines are written, or until a
    # line is read on a day other than +date+; that line is kept for the
    # next file. Lines in an excluded room and lines the mode drops are read
    # and not written, and do not count.
    #
    # @param file [File] the open log file
    # @param date [String] the file's day, "%Y-%m-%d"
    # @return [void]
    def fill_file(file, date)
      written = 0
      while written < @lines_per_file
        line = @carried_line || read_line
        @carried_line = nil
        # The date is checked before anything else, on every line read,
        # excluded or not, so the first line of a new day starts its file.
        if today != date
          @carried_line = line
          break
        end
        excluded = false
        if @track_room
          refresh_room
          refresh_lich_id
          excluded = room_excluded?
          line = decorate_title(line) unless excluded
        end
        # Excluded lines go through the filter too, which may follow a span.
        line = line_text(line)
        next if excluded || line.nil?

        file.puts format_line(line, @clock.now)
        written += 1
      end
    end

    # Works the room out again when the parser has moved on (room count,
    # UID or title changed): whether its UID is excluded and, for --rnum,
    # its title matcher. The Lich number is cleared, for
    # {#refresh_lich_id} to find.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def refresh_room
      return if XMLData.room_count == @room_count && XMLData.room_id == @room_uid && XMLData.room_title == @room_title

      @room_count = XMLData.room_count
      @room_uid = XMLData.room_id
      @room_title = XMLData.room_title.dup
      @room_uid_excluded = @uid_excludes.include?("u#{@room_uid}")
      @room_id_excluded = false
      @lich_id = nil
      @title_pattern = (self.class.room_title_pattern(@room_title, XMLData.game) if @show_room_numbers)
    end

    # Looks the room's Lich number up and checks it against the --exclude
    # Lich numbers, when one is needed and the room's UID is not already
    # excluded. Only a found number is kept for the room. Until then the map
    # is asked again on each line, as it may not be loaded yet or may learn
    # the UID later. While the logger's own map load runs, a found number is
    # asked for again too: the UID table is rebuilt one room at a time, so a
    # UID shared by several rooms can show one.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def refresh_lich_id
      return unless @look_up_lich_id && !@room_uid_excluded && (@lich_id.nil? || @map_loader&.alive?)

      @lich_id = self.class.lich_room_id(@room_uid)
      @room_id_excluded = !@lich_id.nil? && @id_excludes.include?(@lich_id.to_s)
    end

    # Whether the current room is excluded, by its UID or its Lich number.
    #
    # @return [Boolean]
    def room_excluded?
      @room_uid_excluded || @room_id_excluded
    end

    # Starts gzipping this character's old logs in a background thread, at
    # most once a day: when the run opens its first file and when a new
    # day's file opens (see {Logger.compress_old_logs}). The get loop never
    # waits on it, so a sweep over years of logs cannot make Lich's
    # downstream buffer drop lines. It echoes once, with the count and the
    # space, when it compressed something; a sweep still running from the
    # day before is left to finish.
    #
    # @param date [String] the day now, "%Y-%m-%d"
    # @return [void]
    def start_compression(date)
      return if @compressor&.alive?

      # A new day's sweep waits a little, so the other loggers on this
      # character (the XML twin of --both) have closed yesterday's files.
      delay = @compress_date ? DAY_SWEEP_DELAY : 0
      @compress_date = date
      folder = self.class.character_log_folder(XMLData.game, XMLData.name)
      cutoff = Date.strptime(date, '%Y-%m-%d') - @compress_days
      days = @compress_days
      now = @clock.now
      io = @io
      label = self.class.log_label
      @compressor = Thread.new do
        Thread.current.priority = -1
        sleep(delay) if delay.positive?
        count, before, after = self.class.compress_old_logs(folder, cutoff, now: delay.positive? ? Time.now : now)
        if count.positive?
          age = days.zero? ? 'dated before today' : "older than #{days} day#{'s' unless days == 1}"
          io.send(:echo, "compressed #{count} log file#{'s' unless count == 1} #{age}: " \
                         "#{self.class.size_text(before)} down to #{self.class.size_text(after)}.")
        end
      rescue StandardError => e
        Lich.log "#{label}: compressing old logs stopped (#{e.class}: #{e.message})"
      end
    end

    # The day now, as the file names write it.
    #
    # @return [String] "%Y-%m-%d"
    def today
      @clock.now.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')
    end

    # The next game line, from Lich's get (blocks until there is one).
    #
    # @return [String]
    def read_line
      @io.send(:get)
    end
  end

  # The Logger's own Modes, set with const_set; the parse block below
  # documents them (see the note before Mode::TEXT is set).
  fresh::Logger.const_set(:DEFAULT_MODE, mode::TEXT)
  fresh::Logger.const_set(:XML_MODE, mode::XML)
  fresh::Logger.const_set(:XML_STREAMS_MODE, mode::XML_STREAMS)

  # @!parse
  #   class fresh::Logger
  #     # The {Mode} built with this Logger ({Mode::TEXT}), for a run built
  #     # with no mode and for typed commands started with no format. It is
  #     # the class's own, so a run never gets a Mode from another evaluation.
  #     DEFAULT_MODE = Mode::TEXT
  #
  #     # XML mode built with this Logger ({Mode::XML}), see {Logger.mode_for}.
  #     XML_MODE = Mode::XML
  #
  #     # XML mode keeping every line ({Mode::XML_STREAMS}), built with this
  #     # Logger, see {Logger.mode_for}.
  #     XML_STREAMS_MODE = Mode::XML_STREAMS
  #   end

  # Puts this evaluation's Mode and Logger in place of the last ones. Each
  # name is set once, to a whole definition, so it always reads as the old
  # or the new one. Setting a constant again warns unless $VERBOSE is nil,
  # so it is nil for the two assignments only.
  begin
    verbose = $VERBOSE
    $VERBOSE = nil
    const_set(:Mode, mode)
    const_set(:Logger, fresh::Logger)
  ensure
    $VERBOSE = verbose
  end

  # The usage text ;log help shows, one String per line: the commands, every
  # option, where the files go, how to log text and XML together, the
  # DragonRealms yaml timestamp settings and an autostart example, with
  # Lich's command character. Run as logxml (an install that still has
  # logxml.lic, see {Logger.mode_for}), it also says ;logxml is ;log --xml,
  # and that ;log and ;logxml run side by side under their own names.
  #
  # @param name [String] the name the script runs as, e.g. "log"
  # @param char [String] Lich's command character, e.g. ";"
  # @return [Array<String>] the help lines, ready to respond one by one
  def self.help_lines(name, char)
    cmd = "#{char}log"
    pad = ' ' * cmd.length
    xml_name = name == Logger::XML_SCRIPT_NAME
    together =
      if xml_name
        ["  Text and XML together: #{cmd} --both, or #{cmd} and #{char}#{name} side by side (both can be autostarted)."]
      else
        ["  Text and XML together: #{cmd} --both writes both files, and #{char}kill log stops both",
         "  (the XML half runs as #{char}#{Logger::BOTH_XML_NAME}1, which #{char}kill #{Logger::BOTH_XML_NAME}1 stops on its own)."]
      end
    [
      "#{cmd} logs the game to a file.",
      '',
      "  #{cmd}                          game text to a .log file",
      "  #{cmd} --xml                    the raw XML the game sends, to a .xml file",
      "  #{cmd} --both                   both of those at once, as two files",
      ("  #{char}#{name}                       the same as #{cmd} --xml (this install still has #{name}.lic)" if xml_name),
      "  #{cmd} help                     this help",
      '',
      '  Both kinds of log also record script output (what Lich and other scripts echo).',
      '',
      '  Options (text and XML mode alike, unless noted):',
      "  #{pad} --timestamp=\"%F %T %Z\"   start each line with the time, in this strftime format",
      "  #{pad} --rnum                   add the Lich room number to room titles (or --roomnum)",
      "  #{pad} --exclude=u7199,288      skip lines in these rooms: u<uid> for a game room UID, or a",
      "  #{pad}                          Lich room number (needs the room's UID in Lich's map)",
      "  #{pad} --lines=50000            start a new file after this many lines (default 30000)",
      "  #{pad} --streams                XML mode only: keep all the stream window content (very noisy)",
      "  #{pad} --compress-after=7       gzip log files more than this many days old (0: all but today's;",
      "  #{pad}                          in the background, when logging starts and each new day)",
      '',
      "  Files go to Lich's log folder (lich/logs, or the one set with Lich's --logs=), under",
      '  <game>-<character>/<year>/<month>/, with a new file each day and after --lines lines.',
      '',
      *together,
      '',
      '  DragonRealms: log_timestamp: true in your yaml turns timestamps on, in the',
      '  log_timestamp_format format; --timestamp wins over both. log_compress_after: 7',
      '  in your yaml does what --compress-after=7 does; the option wins.',
      '',
      '  A reply that comes within about 50 ms of a typed command can be logged before it.',
      '',
      "  Autostart example: #{char}autostart add --global log --timestamp=\"%F %T %Z\" --rnum"
    ].compact
  end

  # Shows {help_lines} in the game window, for ;log help (or --help), and
  # starts no logging: no file, no want flags, no typed-command thread.
  #
  # @param name [String] the name the script runs as (Script.current.name)
  # @return [void]
  def self.show_help(name = Script.current.name)
    char = $lich_char.to_s.empty? ? ';' : $lich_char
    help_lines(name, char).each { |line| respond(line) }
    nil
  end

  # Starts the XML half of --both: an exec script (Lich's ExecScript, named
  # logxml1 and up, quiet and hidden) that runs {main} in XML mode with this
  # run's options (less --both and help), while this run logs the text. One
  # Lich script gets one downstream buffer, where the text and XML lines
  # would arrive mixed, so each log needs a script of its own. This run's
  # die_with takes the twin down with it (;kill log stops both); ;kill
  # logxml1 stops only the XML log. When Lich does not start the twin, the
  # run says so and logs the text only.
  #
  # @param options [OpenStruct] this run's options, as {Opts.parse} returns
  #   them
  # @return [Object, nil] the started exec script, or nil when none started
  def self.start_xml_twin(options)
    twin_options = options.to_h.reject { |key, _| %i[both help].include?(key) }.merge(xml: true, TWIN_KEY => true)
    twin = ExecScript.start("hide_me\n::#{name}.main(OpenStruct.new(#{twin_options.inspect}))", quiet: true, name: Logger::BOTH_XML_NAME)
    unless twin
      echo '--both: the XML log could not be started (Lich did not start a second script), so only the text log is written.'
      return nil
    end

    Script.current.die_with.push(twin.name)
    twin
  rescue StandardError => e
    echo "--both: the XML log could not be started (#{e.class}: #{e.message}), so only the text log is written."
    nil
  end

  # Runs the logger for this run of the script until the script is killed
  # (or a file error stops it): one +GameLogger::Logger+ with +options+, in
  # the mode `GameLogger::Logger.mode_for` picks from them and the
  # script's name (text mode, or XML mode for --xml or a run named
  # logxml); see +GameLogger::Logger#run+. The Logger class is read once
  # here, so the whole run keeps the one this evaluation set.
  #
  # @example What log.lic runs; ;log --xml --rnum gives these options
  #   GameLogger.main
  #   GameLogger.main(GameLogger::Opts.parse(%w[--xml --rnum]))
  # @param options [OpenStruct] this run's options, as {Opts.parse}
  #   returns them; parsed from the script's arguments when not given
  # @option options [String, true, nil] :timestamp --timestamp=FMT, the
  #   strftime format for line stamps; a bare --timestamp is ignored (see
  #   {timestamp_format})
  # @option options [String, true, nil] :rnum --rnum, or the word rnum:
  #   any value adds room numbers to room titles
  # @option options [String, true, nil] :roomnum --roomnum, the same as
  #   +:rnum+
  # @option options [String, Array<String>, true, nil] :exclude
  #   --exclude=u7199,288: rooms whose lines are not written, by game UID
  #   (u<uid>) or Lich room number (see {exclude_entries})
  # @option options [String, true, nil] :lines --lines=N, the lines
  #   written per file; an invalid count falls back to
  #   {DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE} (see {lines_per_file})
  # @option options [String, true, nil] :xml --xml, or the word xml: any
  #   value picks XML mode, as a run named logxml does
  # @option options [String, true, nil] :streams the word streams or
  #   --streams: in XML mode, any value keeps every line, stream content
  #   too; text mode ignores it
  # @option options [String, true, nil] :both --both, or the word both:
  #   any value logs the text here and the XML in a twin exec script (see
  #   {start_xml_twin})
  # @option options [String, true, nil] :"compress-after" --compress-after=N:
  #   gzip this character's log files dated more than N days ago (see
  #   {compress_after_days}); the log_compress_after setting does the same
  #   when the option is not given
  # @option options [true, nil] :"xml twin of --both" ({TWIN_KEY}) set
  #   only by {start_xml_twin}, which leaves compressing to the text logger
  # @option options [String, true, nil] :help the word help or --help:
  #   shows {help_lines} instead of logging (see {show_help})
  # @return [void]
  # @raise [SystemCallError, IOError] when a log folder or file cannot be
  #   made, opened or written (see +GameLogger::Logger#run+)
  def self.main(options = Opts.parse)
    return show_help if options.help

    start_xml_twin(options) if options.both
    logger = Logger
    logger.new(options, mode: logger.mode_for(options, Script.current.name)).run
  end
end

GameLogger.main
